WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:06.160
Gabriel "FalleN" Toledo is a two-time Major winner, a respected in-game leader and a devastating

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:13.640
AWPer. And, while his success on Counter-Strike's biggest stages alone would be enough to mark him as one of the best players in CS:GO

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:20.780
history, his legacy is built of so much more than in-game results.  This is his story.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:27.240
FalleN was born on May 30th, 1991 and grew up near

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:34.020
Sao Paulo, Brazil. At a young age he gained a love of video games by watching over his brother's shoulder as he played.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:40.200
He was a natural talent in Counter-Strike and by 14 he was already competing on his first team,

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.760
Crashers, in Brazilian CS 1.6

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:45.180
tournaments and World Cyber Games events.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:53.100
A few years later he was asked to join Fire Gamers, whose roster included well-known talents Like Lincoln "fnx" Lau

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.640
and Renato "nak" Nakano.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:01:04.380
FalleN's reputation quickly spread outside of Brazil thanks to impressive individual performances at ESWC and WCG events.

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:10.040
His deadly ability with the AWP marked him as the heir apparent to Raphael "cogu" Camargo

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:13.380
the best player in Brazil and star of MiBR.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:16.600
Thorin: 'What happens is amazingly enough a player comes up

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:22.060
as a rising talent who basically was as close to cogu as you could ever get in play style,

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:27.860
skill set and even like the role that he used in the team it was like a cogu is cogu 2.0.'

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:35.160
However, just as FalleN was coming into his own as a legitimate star-level talent, Counter-Strike in Brazil began to wane.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:42.200
Players became split between 1.6 and the newer Counter-Strike: Source

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.660
While at the same time the upper echelon

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:50.440
competitors of the scene, such as cogu himself, began to retire or move away from CS.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:53.780
But, FalleN's passion for the game was unwavering

00:01:53.780 --> 00:02:00.840
So, he began teaching online Counter-Strike classes, the profits of which he used to create Games Academy in January of

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.880
2012, a school dedicated to teaching

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:05.540
high-level Counter-Strike.

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:10.800
FalleN: 'The main idea of this project is to give you guys a good content on how you can become a better CS:GO player.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:15.760
And we're gonna be talking about a lot of stuff, a lot of tips, a lot of strategies, a lot of thought you need to understand and

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:17.121
how you can improve your game.'

00:02:17.121 --> 00:02:18.740
For the next few years

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:24.960
FalleN continued to grind away, a figurehead around which Brazilian Counter-Strike, rallied but growth was slow,

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:32.460
even after the release of Global Offensive in 2012.  However, once FalleN himself made the switch to CS:GO,

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:35.160
his career quickly regained momentum.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.280
After a stint on Pro Gaming.TD,

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:44.260
FalleN became a founding member of KaBuM! eSports' CS:GO division. The roster, which reunited

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:49.020
FalleN with fnx, also included Fernando "fer" Alvarenga

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:56.500
and the young guns of Lucas "LUCAS1" Teles and Henrique "HEN1" Teles. The move would prove to be a

00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:02.680
significant stepping stone in FalleN's career because it connected him with fer, who has since become his right-hand man.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:10.000
Unfortunately for KaBuM!, their first big international event, ESWC 2014, did not go well.

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:15.280
Their flop, plus a merger between KaBuM! and Pro Gamers.TD,

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:21.920
prompted a roster shakeup leading Into FalleNs' next big test, the MLG Aspen Invitational.

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:28.360
Sadokist: 'Doesn't matter Zqk and steel still get it! It's over just like that! Cloud9 get obliterated

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:34.640
against the Brazilians, KaBuM! I don't think anyone saw that coming and Cloud9 looks stunned. They look speechless.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:40.400
And watch out world - Brazil's got a contender. They look so so so good as a team right now.'

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:47.160
While they didn't make it out of groups, FalleN's preparation and tactics took center stage as KaBuM!TD

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:51.560
dismantled American favorites Cloud9 in an impressive map win.

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:56.280
The performance caught the attention of the community, putting FalleN back on the map.

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:04.360
KaBuM! followed that up with a solid quarterfinal finish at ClutchCon 2015. Their upward trajectory seemed set.

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:12.600
An invitation to the ESL One Katowice 2015 qualifiers was FalleN's reward.  But there was just one problem:

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:14.480
Money

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:20.680
KaBuM!TD couldn't afford to fly the team to Poland for the qualifier.  For a team on the rise,

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:24.600
missing the Major qualifier would be a disaster.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:34.800
FalleN: 'It was fantastic because in five days we raise it all - the money we needed.'

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:40.640
While the community initially came through with around four thousand Euros, it was Fnatic's

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:44.080
Robin "flusha" Ronnquist who was the hero

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:52.220
Donating a 1400 Euro portion of his share of Fnatic's first-place winnings at The IOS Pantamara to the Brazilians.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:56.800
After an additional twenty five hundred dollar donation by ESEA,

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:01.240
KaBuM!TD was able to make their way to Poland in time for the qualifier.

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:09.840
But, successfully qualifying would mean little if they didn't perform at The Major itself, even with a new sponsor, Keyd Stars

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:12.760
Taking the financial worries off the team's mind.

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:16.560
Thankfully, the Brazilians once again showed up in a big way.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:56.000
[casting]

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:00.480
TaZ: 'It's jus the start for [Keyd Stars]. I know they were happy with the score already.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:04.920
Like, with the quarterfinals. But, maybe next tear or next event we can see more of them'

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:10.280
Hiko: 'Still, well played to Keyd. Like, they had a great showing this tournament and I think they kind of proved everyone wrong.

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:13.520
That, you know, nobody else other than American and Europe can actually

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:17.200
play well in these events. They played really well and congratulations to them.'

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:23.280
The top 8 finish was enough to earn the team Legend status and a guaranteed spot in the next Major.

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:28.360
It was a massive weight off of FalleN's shoulders and the culmination of all he had worked for.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:34.360
Brazil, which had been quiet since Mibr's decline was officially back on the radar.

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:42.760
While they had already come so far, it was clear that FalleN and his squad couldn't reach their full capabilities while based in Brazil.

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:47.520
And so, they signed with Luminosity Gaming and moved to California,

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:54.600
where they would be able to train with the best North America had to offer while also having more chances to compete in tournaments.

00:06:54.600 --> 00:07:00.120
The decision was not easy, as it meant being away from his family for an extended period of time.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:03.400
But, it was one that had to be made if FalleN was

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:05.840
to continue to lead his team to the top.

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:11.320
The move to North America also meant adding star player Marcelo "coldzera" David

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:17.000
to the mix, who, under FalleN's guidance, would blossom into one of the best players in the world.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:20.200
[casting]

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:23.760
Another top eight finish at the next Major, ESL One Cologne,

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:25.960
just a month later not only

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:31.520
Proved that the result in Katowice wasn't just a fluke, but that the move to NA had been worth it.

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:38.160
However, he knew there were plenty of talented players back at home in Brazil, waiting for their opportunity to shine.

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:40.080
So, in August of 2015

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:45.120
He held a tournament called 'The Golden Chance'. The winning team he would personally

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:49.062
sponsor under The Game's Academy brand and move them to North America.

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:52.440
Not only would the new team be able to take full advantage of

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:54.440
The NA practice environment,

00:07:54.440 --> 00:08:00.640
FalleN and Luminosity would have a new practice partner just as dedicated and passionate for the game as they were.

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:03.000
The winners were familiar faces:

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:09.920
fnx, Hen1 and Lucas1 along with young talents of Epitacio "TACO" de Melo and

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:14.640
Gustavo "SHOOWTIME" Goncalves. Only a few months into their time in NA,

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:23.520
Luminosity Gaming had improved dramatically. But, they were still bumping against the ceiling presented by European and CIS teams, especially

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:30.600
Fnatic and Natus Vincere. Once again, FalleN needed to take a close look at his team and make a hard choice.

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:37.880
On November 23rd, only a few days before the FACEIT Stage 3 Finals at DreamHack Winter 2015,

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:44.000
FalleN moved fnx and TACO from Games Academy into Luminosity to replace Boltz and steel.

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:48.320
In addition, Zews was brought in as a new coach in place of nak.

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:55.409
The move was sudden, and eyebrows were raised as the Brazilians headed into DreamHack with little practice to speak of.

00:08:55.640 --> 00:08:58.520
They were completely slaughtered in Their first game.

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:09.480
[casting]

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:11.920
But, FalleN wasn't going to let a little

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:17.800
obstacle like an 0-16 beatdown a stop him and his team. In a now legendary performance

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:22.120
Luminosity surged back into the event, defeating Ninjas in Pyjamas...

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:28.720
[casting]

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:29.560
Team EnVyUs...

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:37.200
[casting]

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:38.760
and Team SoloMid...

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:46.440
[casting]

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:51.000
...in dramatic best-of-threes to finish second at the event behind Fnatic.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:10:09.160
[casting]

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:16.320
Something had clicked with the new Luminosity. Wth FalleN and Zews' tactics, fer's consistency,

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:21.540
coldzera's superstar power and the dynamic talents of fnx and TACO, LG

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:25.420
finally had all the pieces they needed to take their game to the next level.

00:10:25.420 --> 00:10:29.840
'If I was now Luminosity, all I would like recall from this tournament is a

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:36.460
crazy comeback. Being able to pick ourselves up even though getting beaten down on the first day one.

00:10:36.460 --> 00:10:39.700
They played different maps against different teams,

00:10:39.700 --> 00:10:47.060
had very very decent result. They just changed two players! I mean they should just look forward and just try to focus on their weaknesses.

00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:49.820
But, this is just something to build on - obviously.'

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:56.560
After second place finishes at DreamHack Leipzig and the IEM World Championship in early 2016,

00:10:56.560 --> 00:11:01.580
Luminosity continued to prove that they had what It took to make a play for the next Major title.

00:11:02.020 --> 00:11:03.980
FalleN was the talk of the town:

00:11:03.980 --> 00:11:08.220
not only was his tactical mind and inspiring leadership a clear

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:14.200
catalyst to LG's success, but he had returned to the level of play that had made him a star in 1.6.

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:20.180
His team played with smarts, skills and limitless passion, making them not only

00:11:20.180 --> 00:11:23.340
incredibly entertaining to watch, but easy to root for.

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:29.220
Luminosity Gaming entered into the MLG Columbus Major in April of 2016 hungry for a title.

00:11:29.220 --> 00:12:24.960
[casting]

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:29.220
Anders: 'And it's Luminosity winning their first Major championship!'

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:36.540
It was one of the most dramatic runs in Major history. FalleN had finally done it: more than ten years after his first

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:41.200
Counter-Strike tournament, he had a restored Brazil to a position of power and glory in the world.

00:12:41.540 --> 00:12:48.020
It was indisputable that LG was the best team in the world, with FalleN their mastermind, their leader.

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:52.740
Redeye: 'Can you sum up to me just what this means to the country of Brazil and your teammates?'

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:58.420
FalleN: 'Just to sum it up this is our dream coming true and winning the biggest tournament ever on CS:GO

00:12:58.640 --> 00:13:02.920
means so much to me personally, and I'm sure it means a kot to my teammates as well.'

00:13:02.920 --> 00:13:05.460
However, their success had not gone unnoticed

00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:07.960
and with that came complications.

00:13:10.660 --> 00:13:17.380
The team became embroiled in a controversial scandal surrounding their sponsor and a potential switch to SK Gaming.

00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:21.660
Despite signing a Letter of Intent to re-sign with Luminosity Gaming,

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:26.320
the team had signed contracts With SK Gaming and just prior to MLG Columbus.

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:33.400
The result was a lengthy public debate and a legal battle over the future of the team, which was eventually

00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:37.860
settled on June 24th, 2016, when the team officially transferred to SK.

00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:42.900
Their first tournament under the SK banner was the ESL One Cologne Major.

00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:47.000
And just as they had at MLG, the Brazilians were gunning to take it all.

00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:51.360
FalleN: 'Our path to get here was very difficult, we had to go through a lot of things.

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:57.260
So, when we have a chance to win another title we're gonna do everything we can to win it. So we're feeling blessed

00:13:57.260 --> 00:14:00.620
to be here and we wanna make a great show and do our best.'

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:02.320
They didn't falter in their mission.

00:14:02.320 --> 00:15:09.340
[casting]

00:15:09.340 --> 00:15:14.320
Anders: 'And SK Gaming - second Major championship in a row! They are your winners!'

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:22.100
Back-to-back Major titles left absolutely no doubt: not only was the roster one of the greatest to ever play CS:GO,

00:15:22.100 --> 00:15:25.440
FalleN was one of the greatest in-game leaders of the modern era.

00:15:26.020 --> 00:15:29.320
FalleN: 'Yeah, I think we found the perfect team. We have been trying for so long.

00:15:29.320 --> 00:15:31.640
I personally have been trying since 2009.

00:15:31.640 --> 00:15:35.960
Seven years trying to achieve tournaments and I have the perfect guys on my side.

00:15:35.960 --> 00:15:39.300
I mean everyone - all of them played so well.'

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:44.240
Heading into the back half of 2016, everyone expected FalleN and SK

00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:49.460
to continue to take home the hardware. But, behind the scenes trouble was brewing.

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:52.260
Fnx, always known as a bad boy,

00:15:52.260 --> 00:15:56.720
was causing tension in the team. While SK's results were nothing to sneeze at,

00:15:56.820 --> 00:16:02.560
they were not were FalleN hoped they would be. He had fought so hard to reach the top and

00:16:02.560 --> 00:16:07.080
anything less than first was a disappointment now that he had tasted victory.

00:16:07.240 --> 00:16:15.340
Fnx was removed from the team and SK Gaming played the ELEAGUE Major in January of 2017 with Ricardo "fox" Pachero

00:16:15.340 --> 00:16:18.360
as a stand-in while the team sought out a new fifth member.

00:16:18.900 --> 00:16:25.860
Proving just how good the team was, even with a stand-in SK Gaming still finished top four at the event.

00:16:25.900 --> 00:16:30.940
Eventually, the up-and-coming star of Immortals, Joao "felps" Vasconcellos

00:16:30.940 --> 00:16:38.500
was selected to join the SK ranks. The team was once again focused, driven and determined to win titles.

00:16:38.500 --> 00:16:41.600
The rest, as they say, is history.

00:16:41.600 --> 00:17:29.040
[casting]

00:17:29.380 --> 00:17:32.680
FalleN's story is one of hard work paying off,

00:17:32.680 --> 00:17:36.520
dedication reaping rewards and passion leading to victory.

00:17:36.740 --> 00:17:43.100
His nickname of 'The Godfather of Brazilian CS' is more than just a moniker - he's earned it.

00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:51.740
Games Academy, which started as just an online class all those years ago,  now boasts its own content creators and has witnessed its

00:17:51.740 --> 00:17:56.840
former roster, now sponsored by Immortals, finish second at the recent PGL Major.

00:17:57.260 --> 00:18:04.860
FalleN was once named "The Most Influential Person in Brazilian Esports" in 2015 for his work building the community,

00:18:04.860 --> 00:18:07.520
teaching and spreading the word of esports.

00:18:20.580 --> 00:18:22.380
He's a two-time Major winner,

00:18:22.380 --> 00:18:27.160
a respected leader, a fierce competitor. In every aspect of his life

00:18:27.160 --> 00:18:33.240
FalleN has gone above and beyond - and something tells us he's just getting started.

00:18:40.760 --> 00:18:45.340
Thanks for Watching if You want more Great content be sure to hit that subscribe button

